WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Allocating and laying out a brand new UI cell for every single row in a massive data set

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.760
instantly destroys your frame rate.

3
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.640
Native iOS development solved this hardware limitation early on with U-TableView,

4
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.880
which keeps an off-screen pool of cells and simply reuses them as you scroll.

5
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.080
React Native's default list component, flat list, attempts to solve that exact same problem,

6
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.040
but instead of native cell reuse, it relies on JavaScript-controlled virtualization.

7
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.720
This diagram shows the core architectural limitation of that approach.

8
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.760
You have a JavaScript thread generating UI nodes and a native UI thread rendering them.

9
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.680
Pushing thousands of list items across the bridge connecting these two threads

10
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.080
creates massive communication overhead.

11
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.800
To fix this, Shopify developed FlashList.

12
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.520
By leveraging React Native's fabric architecture,

13
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.640
FlashList pushes the recycling logic back to the native layer,

14
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.960
mimicking how U-TableView operates.

15
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.840
Achieving a consistent 60 frames per second on complex mobile lists

16
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.280
requires understanding exactly where the memory limits of JavaScript virtualization end

17
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.240
and where the mechanics of native UI recycling take over.

18
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.160
FlatList does not actually recycle native views.

19
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.920
Instead, it aggressively unmounts rows that scroll off screen

20
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.960
and mounts incoming rows to keep its memory footprint low.

21
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.160
This diagram illustrates the render window,

22
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.200
restricting rendering focus to a specific viewport region.

23
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.720
FlatList defaults to a window size of 21,

24
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.320
your visible area, 10 screens above, and 10 below.

25
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.560
As you scroll, rows outside the boundary unmount, releasing memory,

26
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.080
while new items mount ahead.

27
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.760
This creates a very immediate engineering trade-off.

28
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.920
If you shrink the window size down to 5, you aggressively save memory,

29
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.720
but you force the JavaScript thread to execute mount and unmount cycles much more frequently.

30
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.800
You can pair this with the RemoveClipped sub-vumes property,

31
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.320
which detaches off-screen views from the native hierarchy.

32
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.200
It's highly effective for memory management on Android,

33
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.080
though it has a more marginal layout impact on iOS,

34
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.400
where UI-Tet handles view recycling differently.

35
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.200
If you shrink the render window blindly,

36
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.360
you trade memory efficiency for an extreme load on the JS thread.

37
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.560
If you scroll faster than JavaScript can mount new components,

38
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.880
your app's users will just see blank flashes.

39
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.160
Beyond components, flatList has a mathematical bottleneck for fast navigation

40
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.600
and dynamic layout measurement.

41
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.120
This animation demonstrates programmatic scrolling.

42
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.000
If you tell flatList to jump to index 1000,

43
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.640
it executes an O of N operation by default.

44
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.680
The engine sequentially measures the height of every preceding item from the top-down

45
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.440
to know where row 1000 is.

46
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.840
You bypass that measurement pass entirely using get-item layout,

47
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.400
the equivalent to UI kits fixed row heights.

48
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.360
With fixed row heights, flatList calculates offset using simple arithmetic,

49
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.520
index multiplied by row height.

50
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.320
The O of N scan becomes an O of 1 jump directly to your target row.

51
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.480
But there is a strict caveat here.

52
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.200
If your list items contain dynamic content,

53
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.520
like user-supplied text or images with unknown aspect ratios,

54
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.360
this fixed arithmetic fails,

55
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.320
causing flatList to scroll to the wrong positions.

56
00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.760
If your height's vary, but you still need fast navigation,

57
00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.720
you must measure items manually using on layout

58
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.280
and cache results to avoid position errors.

59
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.440
Pre-calculating list layout removes the sequential rendering tax.

60
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.600
For any list with fixed height rows,

61
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.320
it instantly solves scroll navigation lag.

62
00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.320
Even with optimized layout math,

63
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.440
virtualization relies entirely on the execution logic of the React reconciler.

64
00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.800
Here is the most common anti-pattern in React Native lists,

65
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.400
defining inline anonymous functions inside the render item prop.

66
00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.920
Every parent update generates a new memory reference.

67
00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.720
The reconciler interprets this as a changed prop,

68
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.480
needlessly re-rendering every single child component in the list.

69
00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.400
To skip this unnecessary work,

70
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.920
functions must be extracted outside the component body or memoized,

71
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.040
and your list items must be wrapped in React.memo.

72
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.080
Reference stability also extends to the key extractor.

73
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.000
Keys must use stable data identity, never array indexes.

74
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.840
Without a stable key, React cannot map existing component instances

75
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.840
to their updated data positions.

76
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.880
If your list renders heterogeneous item types,

77
00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.480
you have to encode the item type directly into the key.

78
00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.880
Otherwise, the reconciler might try to reuse

79
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:28.240
the wrong component structure for an incompatible data shape.

80
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.080
Virtualization is neutralized if unstable JavaScript references

81
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.800
trick the reconciler into redrawing complex,

82
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.600
unchanging components on every single frame.

83
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.600
Even with perfectly memoized functions

84
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.680
and highly optimized render windows,

85
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.280
flat list permanently bottlenecks on the CPU cost of destroying

86
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.600
and recreating JavaScript components.

87
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.440
This diagram shows flashlist,

88
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.960
which completely bypasses this React unmount lifecycle.

89
00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:56.960
Under true native recycling, actual UI views are never destroyed.

90
00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.880
The empty views loop continuously.

91
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.000
Only the data packets inside swap out as they cycle from bottom

92
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.040
back to the top.

93
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.120
This architectural shift identically mirrors iOS's U-table view.

94
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.600
Since the system no longer allocates new nodes,

95
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.400
the load on your JavaScript thread drops close to zero.

96
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.640
To configure flashlist correctly,

97
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.440
you must provide an estimated item size.

98
00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.560
This allows the engine to accurately size the scroll indicator

99
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.440
without an upfront sequential layout measurement.

100
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.480
But recycling introduces a critical danger.

101
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.680
Local component state persists across data swaps.

102
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.360
Ensure all visual state derives exclusively from your props,

103
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.720
not from internal state that survives the recycling process.

104
00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.360
Shifting workload from JavaScript component destruction

105
00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.120
to a permanent native view pool reduces total CPU overhead,

106
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.080
allowing for higher item complexity without dropping frames.

107
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.880
Flat list is still the correct choice for shorter rays under 100 items,

108
00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.800
static data that rarely changes,

109
00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.600
or projects where minimizing third-party dependencies

110
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.200
is a strict requirement.

111
00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.200
You should migrate to flashlist when the list is the primary UI of the screen,

112
00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.080
handles massive data sets, updates frequently,

113
00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.720
or profiles as a clear frame rate bottleneck.

114
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.000
To verify these gains, profile your list in production.

115
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.600
This graphic compares the flame charts for both architectures.

116
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.880
Use React Dev tools to hunt unmemalized components

117
00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:18.320
appearing every frame.

118
00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.840
Then use XcodeTime profiler to correlate smooth scrolling

119
00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.520
with hidden JS spikes, indicating an oversized window.

120
00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.400
High-performance mobile lists do not happen by default.

121
00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.160
They are engineered by strictly aligning your JavaScript reconciliation logic

122
00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.400
with the hardware's native view architecture.

